The code with the traps handled, verified:

```js
// localStorage — persistent, string-only, synchronous
const save = (key, val) => {
  try {
    localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(val));
  } catch (e) {
    // QuotaExceededError or private-mode rejection — never assume writes succeed
    console.warn('persist failed', e);
  }
};
const load = (key, fallback) => {
  try {
    const raw = localStorage.getItem(key);
    return raw === null ? fallback : JSON.parse(raw);   // parse AFTER null check
  } catch {
    return fallback;                                    // corrupted value ≠ crash
  }
};

// sessionStorage — identical API, per-TAB lifetime
sessionStorage.setItem('wizard-step', '2');

// CROSS-TAB SYNC — the free message channel
window.addEventListener('storage', (e) => {
  // fires in OTHER tabs of the same origin when a key changes:
  // e.key, e.oldValue, e.newValue — logout in one tab can react everywhere
  if (e.key === 'theme') applyTheme(e.newValue);
});
// NOTE: does NOT fire in the tab that WROTE the value.

// cookies — no object API: document.cookie is a serialized string
(document.cookie = 'theme=dark; path=/; max-age=31536000; samesite=lax');
// real cookie SETTING belongs on Set-Cookie response headers when possible
```

The selection logic (say it as a decision): server-owned + rides requests → cookie (HttpOnly when the server owns it); client-owned + survives restarts → localStorage; client-owned + tab-scoped → sessionStorage; big or offline or queryable → IndexedDB (async — Promise wrapper or idb-keyval; transactions keep multi-key writes consistent).

The event details worth knowing: the storage event also fires for sessionStorage changes within the SAME tab's OTHER iframes/contexts — the sync boundary is the browsing-context tree, not just cross-tab; and the broadcast is one-way (writer excluded) — the reason a 'sync now' pattern pairs setItem with a manual dispatch in the writing tab.
